let contentPages = [];

// Abstract
contentPages.push("<h2>1. ABSTRACT</h2><p>The College Management System aims to simplify, automate, and streamline the extensive administrative, academic, and financial operations within a college environment. Traditional campus administration heavily relies on disparate paper-based ledgers, manual data entry, and fragmented computing resources, resulting in massive data redundancy, communication gaps between departments, and inevitable human errors. Consequently, institutional efficiency plummets while administrative overhead skyrockets.</p><p>This web-based ERP (Enterprise Resource Planning) software introduces an integrated, robust digital infrastructure utilizing classical, highly reliable web technologies: Hypertext Markup Language (HTML), Cascading Style Sheets (CSS), PHP (PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor), and MySQL relational databases. This stack ensures profound cross-platform compatibility, requiring only a modern web browser on the client-side, while executing powerful server-side parsing and database querying securely via the Apache server.</p><p>Core functionalities engineered into the system include secure Multi-Tier Authentication mapping distinct User Interfaces to specific organizational roles (Administrator, Faculty, and Student). Key modules encompass comprehensive Student Registration and Profiling, automated Attendance and Continuous Assessment tracking, secure Financial and Fee transaction logging, and real-time Notice Board broadcasts.</p>");
contentPages.push("<h2>1. ABSTRACT (CONTINUED)</h2><p>The system fundamentally guarantees Data Integrity through rigid MySQL relational schemas mapping Primary and Foreign Key constraints structurally across the entire campus dataset. Furthermore, implementing PHP session algorithms ensures absolute protection against unverified access attempts via URL injection attacks.</p><p>By migrating from manual data repositories into this unified RDBMS (Relational Database Management System) web platform, the institution drastically accelerates data retrieval speeds, facilitates absolute transparency among stakeholders, and drastically reduces its carbon footprint by digitizing extensive paper trails. Conclusively, the College Management System redefines institutional operations converting sluggish procedures into automated, instantaneous web transactions thereby augmenting global administrative productivity.</p>");

// Intro
contentPages.push("<h2>2. INTRODUCTION</h2><p>Education represents the cornerstone of modern civilization's advancement. Managing educational institutions—specifically large-scale colleges and universities—requires the massive coordination of thousands of individuals, intricate academic schedules, complex financial streams, and stringent physical resource allocation. Historically, these massive logistical requirements were overseen by armies of clerks manually scribing data across vast physical archives.</p><p>The advent of accessible Web 2.0 technologies fundamentally transformed organizational possibilities. The College Management System explicitly harnesses this digital revolution, aiming to completely digitize the campus biosphere. The project leverages the proven LAMP/WAMP (Linux/Windows, Apache, MySQL, PHP) stack to construct a highly responsive, perpetually active Web Application facilitating all vital institutional parameters over standard HTTP/HTTPS protocols.</p><p>Within this paradigm, a robust central server acts as the primary logical node. Clients—whether they are administrators situated in the institutional head office, faculty operating from departmental staff rooms, or students accessing the portal from their mobile devices globally—interact exclusively through standard web browsers. The server executes complex PHP scripts mathematically manipulating localized data repositories maintained gracefully by the MySQL database daemon, delivering dynamic HTML DOM constructs back across the network.</p>");

contentPages.push("<h3>2.1 Need of the System</h3><p>The absolute necessity for deploying a specialized software infrastructure within the campus operational grid is verified by observing the severe limitations inherently restricting the incumbent manual methodologies.</p><ul><li><strong>Eradicating Data Redundancy:</strong> Without central databases, identical student data is copied physically across library, finance, and academic ledgers. Updating one ledger rarely propagates effectively to others.</li><li><strong>Expediting Data Retrieval:</strong> Locating a specific student's payment record from three years prior manually requires hours traversing physical archives. A SQL query executes the identical retrieval task in a fraction of a millisecond.</li><li><strong>Physical Integrity and Security:</strong> Analog files are incredibly susceptible to catastrophic environmental destruction (fire, water damage) and unauthorized physical extraction. Digital databases facilitate automated redundant global backups and strict encrypted password access logic.</li><li><strong>Real-time Communication:</strong> Crucial academic updates broadcast via physical notice boards suffer massive propagation delays. A digital dashboard instantly beams critical institutional changes to every registered stakeholder contemporaneously.</li></ul>");

contentPages.push("<h3>2.2 Objectives</h3><p>The rigorous engineering efforts invested within this project are bounded by the following explicit primary objectives:</p><ol><li>To synthesize a Graphical User Interface (GUI) via HTML/CSS that is profoundly intuitive minimizing software training requirements drastically for non-technical administrative staff.</li><li>To construct a scalable relational database architecture deploying MySQL structurally ensuring normalization paradigms preventing insert/update/delete anomalies universally.</li><li>To implement strict PHP Server-Side Validation logic guaranteeing that malicious SQL injection strings or logically incorrect data arrays are vehemently rejected preceding database insertion.</li><li>To drastically accelerate the institutional report generation lifecycle dynamically converting raw tabulated data into structured academic prints immediately upon demand.</li></ol><h3>2.3 Scope</h3><p>The functional boundaries marking the system's operational envelope currently encompass:</p><ul><li><strong>Admissions and Identity Management:</strong> Digitizing student intake, allocating unique registration IDs, and maintaining expansive demographic profiles.</li><li><strong>Academic Faculty Tools:</strong> Empowering educators dynamically manipulating class attendance metrics and recording continuous assessment (internal examination) parameters per assigned subject codes.</li><li><strong>Financial Ledgers:</strong> Automating the calculation of levied tuition fees against verified remittance receipts tracking precise outstanding balances algebraically per student node.</li></ul>");

// Expanded Theory to hit 70 pages minimum
contentPages.push("<h3>2.4 Justification of Chosen Technology Stack</h3><p>The selection of the LAMP/WAMP stack (Linux/Windows, Apache, MySQL, PHP) was not arbitrary but deeply calculated based on institutional needs and constraints. PHP remains the undisputed leader in server-side scripting for web applications due to its tight integration with HTML and extensive documentation. Its procedural and object-oriented paradigms allow for rapid prototyping and robust software engineering.</p><p>MySQL is leveraged for its ACID (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) compliance. In a college environment where financial transactions (fee payments) and permanent academic records (grades) are processed daily, data corruption is catastrophic. MySQL guarantees that transactions are completed fully or rolled back entirely, ensuring that the database never rests in an invalid state. This level of transactional security is strictly required for enterprise-grade management software.</p><p>Furthermore, Apache HTTP Server is deployed because of its pervasive global infrastructure, capability to handle thousands of concurrent requests via Multi-Processing Modules (MPMs), and deep modularity (e.g., mod_rewrite for clean URL structures ensuring SEO and security). The cohesive synergy of these technologies provides a highly secure, tremendously fast, and perfectly stable basis for the College Management System.</p>");

// System Analysis
contentPages.push("<h2>3. SYSTEM ANALYSIS</h2><p>Systems Analysis represents the critical investigatory phase preceding software construction wherein the system analyst intensely evaluates the operational intricacies defining the current workflow logic and subsequently drafts the architectural blueprint defining the proposed computational alternative.</p><h3>3.1 Existing System</h3><p>The incumbent operational methodology relies exclusively upon physical analog technologies and disjointed local executable software (e.g., isolated MS Excel spreadsheets operating on standalone desktop computers disconnected from any central area network).</p><p><strong>Drawbacks of the Existing System:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Massive Operational Delay:</strong> Transferring physical documents between the Finance block and the Principal's office introduces multi-day latency periods rendering rapid decision making impossible.</li><li><strong>Human Transcription Error:</strong> Repeatedly copying numeric sequences manually strictly guarantees mathematical variances structurally corrupting institutional data validity.</li><li><strong>Security and Access Restrictions:</strong> Spreadsheets lack granular access protocols. If a clerk possesses physical access to the machine, they theoretically possess unmitigated capability altering sensitive financial records bypassing authorization validation.</li><li><strong>No External Accessibility:</strong> Students inherently possess zero visibility concerning their internal status physically requiring them to queue extensively outside administrative blocks merely determining minor informational updates like outstanding fees.</li></ul>");
contentPages.push("<h3>3.2 Proposed System</h3><p>The Proposed System completely overhauls the operational infrastructure by deploying a centralized Web-Based Client-Server architecture. The MySQL server securely harbors all institutional information centrally. End-users interact exclusively via PHP-driven dynamic web interfaces guaranteeing data uniformity globally.</p><p><strong>Advantages of the Proposed System:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Unified Central Data Repository:</strong> Absolute data consistency is mathematically enforced globally. If an address is updated at the Registrar terminal, that specific coordinate string updates simultaneously for the Library and Academic terminals executing zero latency.</li><li><strong>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):</strong> The PHP Session controller rigorously executes identity validation. A student login possesses exclusively read-only 'Select' privileges viewing only their specific row data, whereas an Admin login receives extensive 'Update/Delete' execution capability universally.</li><li><strong>Algorithmic Validation:</strong> The script dynamically evaluates data before storage, natively blocking mathematically impossible entries (e.g., attempting to record attendance on a date mapped within the future).</li><li><strong>Global Accessibility:</strong> Unbounded by institutional physical geography, authorized entities accessing the public IP address via internet infrastructure manipulate operational capabilities 24/7 constantly.</li></ul>");
contentPages.push("<h3>3.3 Feasibility Analysis</h3><p>To assure project success, comprehensive feasibility tests were executed evaluating the three primary tenets of software deployment viabilities: Technical, Economic, and Operational metrics.</p><p><strong>Technical Feasibility:</strong> The required technologies (PHP/MySQL) are open-source and natively supported across virtually any computing hardware. Developing the system requires no exotic proprietary tooling. Modern browsers natively support the HTML5/CSS3 frontend rendering required. Thus, technical feasibility is absolutely verified.</p><p><strong>Economic Feasibility:</strong> The financial impact of migrating to an open-source stack is tremendously low. Unlike enterprise C# .NET or Oracle deployments requiring massive licensing fees, PHP and MySQL incur zero capital expenditure for software licenses. The server hosting costs are heavily outweighed by the reduction in paper archives and administrative labor wastage.</p><p><strong>Operational Feasibility:</strong> The interface is designed utilizing standard web methodologies analogous to common internet platforms. The learning curve for administrative staff is exceptionally shallow. Operational acceptance is guaranteed due to the immediate reduction in manual workload the system inherently provides to the faculty and clerical operators.</p>");

// Requirements
contentPages.push("<h2>4. SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS</h2><p>Software deployment mandates specific minimum physical and software operational environments validating execution parameters precisely. These prerequisites dictate the fundamental foundation operationalizing the PHP compiler and the MySQL database daemon reliably.</p><h3>4.1 Hardware Requirements</h3><p>Due to the centralized server topology implemented, the Client-side hardware requisite is fundamentally minimal necessitating only sufficient processing capability executing a modern web browser engine securely.</p><p><strong>Server-Side Hardware:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Processor:</strong> Intel Core i5 / AMD Ryzen 5 or higher executing a minimum 2.5 GHz clock cycle.</li><li><strong>Memory (RAM):</strong> 8 GB DDR4 Minimum (16GB Recommended for maintaining large concurrent MySQL connection pools).</li><li><strong>Storage:</strong> 256 GB SSD (Solid State Drive) accelerating Database read/write IOPS extensively.</li></ul><p><strong>Client-Side Hardware:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Processor:</strong> Dual-Core CPU (Intel Pentium / Core i3 or equivalent mobile ARM architecture).</li><li><strong>Memory:</strong> 2 GB RAM (Sufficient parsing DOM structures efficiently).</li><li><strong>Interface:</strong> Minimum 1024x768 Display resolution.</li></ul>");
contentPages.push("<h3>4.2 Software Requirements</h3><p>The operational logic strictly demands the LAMP/XAMPP stack environments parsing specific syntax.</p><ul><li><strong>Operating System:</strong> Platform Agnostic (Windows 10/11, Ubuntu Linux, macOS). Web Servers operate interchangeably.</li><li><strong>Web Server Engine:</strong> Apache HTTP Server v2.4+ (Executing HTTP routing and PHP CGI wrapping).</li><li><strong>Scripting Preprocessor:</strong> PHP v7.4 or PHP 8.x (Required parsing complex object-oriented procedural syntax).</li><li><strong>Database Engine:</strong> MySQL Community Server v5.7+ or MariaDB equivalent (Executing internal SQL relation operations).</li><li><strong>Client User Agent:</strong> Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Apple Safari (Any ECMAScript 6 compliant web browser).</li><li><strong>Development IDE:</strong> Visual Studio Code, Sublime Text, or Notepad++.</li></ul><h3>4.3 Front End Description (HTML/CSS)</h3><p>The Front-End represents the singular visual interface bounding human-machine interaction. It masks complex server routines behind intuitive graphical layouts structured primarily by HTML and styled via CSS.</p><h4>Hypertext Markup Language (HTML)</h4><p>HTML essentially dictates the structural backbone defining the physical layout encompassing the graphical elements displayed via the browser canvas. Employing modern HTML5 specifications enables utilizing semantic tags rendering the application highly readable technically ensuring supreme accessibility compliance.</p>");
contentPages.push("<h4>Cascading Style Sheets (CSS)</h4><p>Pure HTML generates visually unappealing, strictly linear data presentations. CSS logically bifurcates the aesthetic styling from the structural HTML logic enabling extensive graphical customization. By addressing elements via DOM Selectors (Classes and IDs), CSS defines exact colorimetry, precise pixel typography adjustments, absolute positioning coordinates, and complex Flexbox/Grid alignment parameters.</p><p>Crucially, this application heavily employs CSS Media Queries facilitating Responsive Web Design (RWD) architecture. Media queries algebraically interrogate the client device screen dimensions dynamically reconfiguring the CSS logical layout guaranteeing the Administrative dashboard remains structurally usable independently evaluating whether rendered on a 1080p Desktop Monitor or a 4-inch Mobile Touchscreen device securely.</p><h4>JavaScript (Client-Side Logic)</h4><p>While PHP handles absolute Server logic, JavaScript executes lightweight Client-Side validations prior to form submission. For example, JavaScript rigorously evaluates password length constraints and regex-validates email formats matching required syntax directly inside the browser saving valuable Server processing execution cycles and drastically accelerating instantaneous User UI feedback.</p>");

contentPages.push("<h3>4.4 Back End Description (PHP/MySQL)</h3><p>The Back-End dictates the absolute core intellectual logic comprising the application. Situated securely on the central Server, it intercepts incoming Client payloads, evaluates conditions, and physically manipulates permanent data objects respectively.</p><h4>PHP (Hypertext Preprocessor)</h4><p>PHP is an immensely powerful Server-Side scripting language engineered distinctly evaluating dynamic web development parameters. Unlike HTML residing statically on the Client, PHP code execution completes entirely upon the Server. The resulting output dynamically constructing pure HTML string payloads beamed to the user ensuring source code logic remains completely obfuscated shielding proprietary algorithms against malicious visual inspection universally.</p><p>Within the College System topology, PHP undertakes monumental operational responsibilities involving:</p><ul><li><strong>Session Management:</strong> Utilizing `$_SESSION` superglobals ensuring authentication tokens persist across separate web pages securely identifying specifically logged-in entities mathematically.</li><li><strong>Database Bridging:</strong> PHP employs the PDO (PHP Data Objects) or `mysqli` extension effectively acting as the conduit transmitting explicit raw SQL statement queries deeply into the MySQL core execution engine interpreting subsequent mathematical row arrays returned logically.</li><li><strong>Data Sanitization:</strong> Preceding database interactions, PHP forcefully sanitizes incoming `$_POST` variables stripping malicious escape characters thereby executing total immunity guarding against disastrous Server vulnerabilities specifically SQL-Injection vectors.</li></ul>");
contentPages.push("<h4>MySQL (Relational Database)</h4><p>MySQL is the globally ubiquitous Open-Source Relational Database Management System (RDBMS) utilizing Structured Query Language (SQL) commanding massive unstructured data clusters structuring them efficiently.</p><p>MySQL inherently organizes internal operational records structurally within 'Tables' encompassing rigorously typed vertical 'Columns' and horizontally accumulating 'Rows' (Records). Crucial specific MySQL features leveraging this project architecture involve:</p><ul><li><strong>Primary Keys:</strong> Executing unique integer AUTO_INCREMENT properties allocating absolute uniqueness identifiers differentiating identically named student entities perfectly via numeric vectors (e.g., student_id = 405).</li><li><strong>Foreign Keys (Referential Integrity):</strong> Structurally binding Tables together. The `fees` table references the `students` table via the student_id Primary Key thereby ensuring 'Orphan Records' algorithmically cannot exist ensuring absolute data topological correlation logically.</li><li><strong>Complex SQL Joins:</strong> MySQL rapidly executing `INNER JOIN` syntax dynamically reconstructing massive customized multi-table data views rapidly displaying complex relationships seamlessly upon the Admin Dashboard globally.</li></ul>");
contentPages.push("<h4>Advanced Database Operations</h4><p>Beyond elementary CRUD functionality, the backend aggressively implements algorithmic SQL aggregate procedures grouping datasets temporally and structurally. Functions including `SUM()`, `COUNT()`, and `AVG()` are manipulated centrally allowing Administrators comprehensive macro-vantage insights determining demographic metrics instantly. Indexing `student_id` vectors across high-traffic lookup instances accelerates structural access drastically converting O(n) algorithmic scan speeds computationally to near O(1) hashing efficiencies dynamically preserving network integrity extensively under severe HTTP throughput load conditions.</p>");

// SDLC
contentPages.push("<h2>5. SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT LIFE CYCLE (SDLC)</h2><p>The Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) defines the rigorous systematic engineering frameworks assuring massive complex software projects transit methodically from conceptualization arriving structurally sound, mathematically correct deployable platforms. This project critically adheres modeling the traditional <strong>Waterfall Methodology</strong> sequentially executing structured sequential phases reliably.</p><h3>5.1 Requirement Gathering and Analysis</h3><p>The foundational phase requiring intense collaborative dialogue investigating the precise administrative boundaries defining currently utilized campus operations. Analysts meticulously generated the Software Requirements Specification (SRS) document explicitly detailing algorithmic limits, User role capabilities definitively enumerating functional inputs mapping explicit mathematical output bounds strictly.</p><h3>5.2 System Design</h3><p>Translating theoretical SRS specifications directly constructing technical blueprints evaluating the systematic logic. This sequence produces Data Flow Diagrams (DFDs) tracing string path routes mapping procedural connections. Concurrently, Entity-Relationship (ER) diagrams determine complex MySQL table normalization boundaries defining foreign key constraint intersections mapping the database architectural shell fundamentally.</p>");
contentPages.push("<h3>5.3 Implementation (Coding Phase)</h3><p>The longest iterative engineering phase involving the physical transcription transforming abstracted logical flowcharts evaluating explicit PHP scripting syntaxes and corresponding MySQL table generations structurally. The codebase is heavily modularized; complex operational requirements are logically decoupled yielding isolated singular functional blocks effectively preventing cross-module code contamination errors maintaining deep systematic organizational structure.</p><h3>5.4 Integration and Testing</h3><p>Post-compilation, independent modular execution blocks natively evaluate integration connectivity parameters specifically. Systemic Quality Assurance engineers vigorously test the application systematically injecting malformed strings determining robustness, traversing authentication loops repeatedly ensuring session tracking boundaries effectively prevent privilege escalation maneuvers computationally.</p><h3>5.5 Deployment and Maintenance</h3><p>Deploying validated codebase constructs transferring operations onto live operational Server architectures. Subsequent maintenance involves actively logging runtime diagnostic variables dynamically determining performance bottleneck attributes sequentially initiating micro-code surgical updates structurally optimizing execution query vectors indefinitely extending the software's operational lifespan seamlessly.</p>");
contentPages.push("<h3>5.6 Risk Management Mitigation</h3><p>Risk mitigation defines identifying disastrous engineering failures initiating preventative contingencies mitigating catastrophic system loss. Establishing automated daily MySQL logical dumps mitigates physical disk degradation. Furthermore implementing explicit HTTPS SSL/TLS execution wrappers eliminates 'Man in the Middle' packet sniffer attacks encrypting sensitive credentials transversing internet networks completely guaranteeing total user security intrinsically validating strict enterprise compliance globally.</p>");

// DFD
contentPages.push("<h2>6. DATA FLOW DIAGRAM (DFD)</h2><p>A Data Flow Diagram (DFD) structurally illustrates the holistic systemic flow mapping information propagation dynamically moving transversally across the operational boundaries. DFDs entirely abstract complex programmatic control loops focusing exquisitely tracing data generation origins tracking modifications iteratively documenting final repository persistence locations logically.</p><h3>6.1 Context Level Diagram (Level 0 DFD)</h3><p>The Context Diagram provides macro-level observability defining the entire College Management System as a singular central operative node interacting explicitly interacting bounded external entity parameters globally.</p><ul><li><strong>Admin Entity:</strong> Transmits Configuration Data, Student Registration vectors, and validates Systemic Outputs generating definitive Data objects injecting directly modifying the central node.</li><li><strong>Faculty Entity:</strong> Injects internal examination score sets, manipulates periodic Subject Attendance data arrays securely parsing inputs evaluating specific bounds globally.</li><li><strong>Student Entity:</strong> Retrieves structured Report structures requesting Transcript readouts pulling Read-Only data paths traversing outbound directly extracted from the core processing node securely.</li></ul>");
